            "https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/graph-api",
            "https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/graph-api",
            "https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/graph-api",
            "https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/graph-api",
            "https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/graph-api"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answer": "In LangGraph, a **checkpointer** is used to save and restore the state of a graph execution, particularly at super-step boundaries. It ensures that if execution is interrupted or needs to be resumed, the graph can continue from where it left off without re-executing completed tasks. Specifically, the checkpointer:\n\n1. **Saves task results**: When a node contains multiple tasks, the checkpointer stores the results of completed tasks, allowing resumed threads to skip re-executing them.\n2. **Handles interrupts and retries**: If execution stops due to an interrupt or retry, the checkpointer ensures the affected node restarts from the beginning, re-running code and side effects before the pause.\n3. **Supports graph migrations**: The checkpointer enables seamless migrations of graph definitions (nodes, edges, and state) while maintaining state tracking.\n\n**Citations**:\n- [Graph API overview](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/graph-api)",
      "citations": [
