        },
        {
          "name": "langchain_docs_tool",
          "args": {
            "input": "tool-calling loop"
          },
          "round": 1,
          "sources": [
            "https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/models",
            "https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/models",
            "https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/tools",
            "https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/models",
            "https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/models"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answer": "Based on the provided evidence, here\u2019s how a **LangGraph StateGraph** differs from a **LangChain agent's tool-calling loop**:\n\n1. **State Management**:\n   - **StateGraph**: Manages state explicitly through defined schemas (`InputState`, `OutputState`, `OverallState`, etc.). Nodes can read from and write to specific state channels, allowing for fine-grained control over data flow. For example, a node can write to a `PrivateState` channel for internal communication. (Source: Graph API overview)\n   - **LangChain Agent**: Relies on a tool-calling loop where the agent invokes tools based on the model's response. The state is implicitly managed through the conversation history and tool results, which are passed back to the model for further reasoning. (Source: Models)\n\n2. **Tool Invocation**:\n   - **StateGraph**: Tools are invoked as nodes in the graph. When a `ToolNode` is added, it accesses the current graph state. Custom state fields can be passed explicitly if needed. (Source: Workflows and agents)\n   - **LangChain Agent**: Tools are invoked based on the model's decision during the conversation. The agent handles the tool execution loop, passing results back to the model for further processing. (Source: Models)\n\n3. **Graph Structure**:\n   - **StateGraph**: Defines a structured graph with nodes and edges, allowing for complex workflows. Nodes can write to private or overall state channels, enabling modular and reusable components. (Source: Graph API overview)\n   - **LangChain Agent**: Follows a linear tool-calling loop, where the model generates tool calls, tools are executed, and results are passed back to the model. There is no explicit graph structure. (Source: Models)\n\n4. **Schema Flexibility**:\n   - **StateGraph**: Supports explicit input and output schemas, allowing nodes to write to state channels not included in their input schema. Private state channels can be defined for internal communication. (Source: Graph API overview)\n   - **LangChain Agent**: Does not have explicit schema definitions for state management. Tool inputs and outputs are handled implicitly through the conversation history. (Source: Models)\n\n**Cited Sources**:\n- [Workflows and agents](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/workflows-agents)\n- [Graph API overview](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/graph-api)\n- [Models](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/models)",
