      "answer": "In LangGraph, a checkpointer is used to save and restore the state of a graph, including completed task results, to ensure determinism and idempotency when resuming execution after an interrupt or retry. It saves checkpoints at super-step boundaries, not mid-function inside a node. This allows for re-execution of nodes from the start of their function, ensuring that code and side effects before the pause run again. Checkpointers also enable graph migrations, handling changes to graph definitions (nodes, edges, and state) even when tracking state. (https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/graph-api)",
